From 07a958478cd2574472b8313c4884156128d10e4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: l4h <l4h@localhost>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 19:30:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] x86: AMD-V (SVM) behind the VCPU interface

On an AMD CPU, a kernel built with KernelVTX now runs VCPUs with SVM:
the VCPU object holds a VMCB where it holds a VMCS on Intel, the EPT
objects hold nested page tables, and the same capabilities and VMEnter
serve guests.  vtx_init() picks by the CPU's vendor, so one image runs
VMs on both.  32-bit kernels keep VT-x only.

- Each core needs SVM not disabled in VM_CR, nested paging, next-RIP
  saving and ASIDs; without them the kernel boots without VMs, as patch
  0038 does for VT-x, and SKIM builds leave SVM off too, as do builds
  without KernelX86_64VTX64BitGuests: restore_svm() switches r8-r15,
  which the VCPU then does not hold.  EFER.SVME is
  set on every core, and the unused VMXON region is the host save area.
- ReadVMCB reads up to 64 words of the VMCB, and WriteVMCB writes one,
  by byte offset.  Userspace may write only the fields listed in
  svm_field(), and only some of their bits.  The kernel keeps on the
  intercepts the host needs: INTR, NMI, SMI, INIT and shutdown, INVD
  (which would discard dirty cache lines of seL4 and other guests), the
  permission maps (every port exits, and only the MSRs VMLOAD and
  VMSAVE switch do not), VMRUN, VMLOAD, VMSAVE, CLGI, STGI, SKINIT and
  INVLPGA, which would act on host state or host physical memory in a
  guest, XSETBV (XCR0 takes only values WriteMSR checked), RDPMC, RDPRU, MONITOR, MWAIT
  and every debug register access, and #DB, #AC (a guest could loop on
  them) and #MC.  The permission map addresses, nCR3, the ASID, AVIC,
  SEV and the virtual VMLOAD and GIF settings are the kernel's; words
  with host physical addresses read as 0.  CR4 writes always exit, and
  CR4 takes only the bits in VCPU_GUEST_CR4_ALLOWED (vcpu.h), whose
  effects stay in guest mode: others enable state nothing switches,
  such as PKRU, which the next guest on the core would run with, and
  bits later CPUs define stay clear too.  Addresses VMLOAD loads as
  they are must be canonical, and gPAT valid (seL4_InvalidArgument).
  ReadVMCS, WriteVMCS and the I/O port invocations are refused on AMD,
  and ReadVMCB and WriteVMCB on Intel (seL4_IllegalOperation).
- An invocation on a VCPU whose thread is in guest mode on another core
  first takes it out of guest mode there, as switchVCPU() does on VT-x:
  the CPU owns a VMCB during VMRUN, and the exit writes the VCPU before
  it takes the kernel lock.  vcpu_make_current() (patch 0039) records the
  VCPU as its core's before that core releases the lock to enter guest
  mode, so none is missed.  A VCPU being deleted is forgotten on every
  core it ran on, not only the last.  So it is when a thread loses its
  VCPU in guest mode and its VMEnter ends (patch 0039).
- VMEnter has VT-x's MR layout, with SVM's fields in it (vmenter.h):
  the misc1 intercepts, EVENTINJ (its defined bits, as WriteVMCB
  takes), the exit code, EXITINFO1 and 2, the
  instruction length from NEXT_RIP, the interrupt state, CR3 and
  EXITINTINFO.  ReadMSR and WriteMSR reach the syscall MSRs,
  KERNEL_GS_BASE and CR2 in the VMCB, and the VCPU's XCR0 (patch
  0039), which is loaded around VMRUN as around a VT-x entry.
- restore_svm() runs the guest with CLGI, STI, VMLOAD, VMRUN, then
  VMSAVE and a VMLOAD of the host's FS, GS, TR, LDTR and syscall MSRs,
  which each core saves the first time it runs a VCPU; only then, on
  the kernel stack, STGI.  The exit finds the save area's address in
  the VCPU's SVM state, not in its register array, which WriteRegisters
  and WriteVMCB write.  IF set at VMRUN is what makes a host
  interrupt end guest mode, with V_INTR_MASKING always on.  The STI
  comes right after CLGI, not just before VMRUN: a VMRUN that exits
  while delivering EVENTINJ would leave the STI shadow in the guest's
  INT_STATE.  An NMI
  that arrived in guest mode is then taken in the kernel, which ignores
  it there instead of halting, and only there: elsewhere it may have
  come in another trap's frame or with the user's GS.  The exit goes on as a VT-x one does, through
  c_handle_vmexit(): INTR, NMI, SMI and INIT go back into the guest
  after the host has handled them, and the rest are faults to userspace.
  After a shutdown, whose guest state is undefined, the save area is
  reset.
- Nested page table entries have the long-mode format: ept.c builds
  EPT entries and stores them converted (nptFromEPT()), with user
  access at every level, as the CPU's nested walks are user accesses.
  What ept.c reads back, the present bit, the page size and the
  address, is in the same place in both.  The memory type becomes an
  index into the host's PAT: write-combining uses its WC entry.
- TLB entries are tagged by ASIDs, which are per core.  A VCPU takes
  the next ASID of its core, and an invalidation (unmapping from a
  nested page table, FlushTLB, a new root) moves that core to a new
  generation, so every VCPU takes a fresh ASID before it runs there
  again.  A VCPU that moves to another core takes a fresh ASID there
  too, which is what VT-x's VPID flush on a move does.  invept() reaches the same cores as on VT-x.  When the ASIDs
  run out, every entry on the core flushes them all until a VMRUN that
  passed its checks has.
- The CPU may keep VMCB fields from the last VMRUN of a VMCB on a core
  (clean bits).  The kernel clears the bits of the fields each write
  changes, and all of them when the VCPU last ran on another core or
  another VCPU ran there since.  A VMRUN that failed its checks may not
  have loaded the changed fields, so they stay dirty for the next.
